	"notes": "Figma's homepage is a stack of full-width marketing sections — a hero, a couple of feature grids, a scrolling showcase, a footer — and inside every one of those sections the actual content is capped at about 1520px and centred, so a huge monitor mostly just adds empty space on the sides rather than showing more. The one section that behaves differently on purpose is a row of seven example cards further down the page: instead of wrapping to a new row or shrinking to fit, those cards are laid out wider than the browser window itself at every desktop size tested, so the visitor is meant to drag or scroll sideways through them — a deliberately different kind of 'row that doesn't fit' than the grids around it. On a phone, the two-up quote row and the four-up feature grid both drop to a single column and the header's links collapse into one hamburger icon.",
